    para('The primary question is answered by a predominance of lexical errors in the open-ended corpus. Grammatical construction and morphological form errors also occur, particularly in the Gemini- and Claude-labelled groups. GPT has the lowest observed rate and differs from both groups in paired comparisons. Gemini and Claude cannot be reliably ordered by the specified primary test. The data therefore identify error patterns and conditional group differences for this prompt set. They provide limited evidence about exact commercial model versions, future model releases, or all Kazakh writing. Agreement and recall values require the additional qualification that the original independent human labels were not preserved in the supplied materials.')
    heading('Discussion')
    para('The central finding is that low aggregate error rates coexist with identifiable local weaknesses. Many responses have no primary candidate, yet technical word choice, case government and sentence construction produce recurring problems. The normalized ranking and the response prevalence tell a consistent story for GPT comparisons. The Gemini–Claude contrast is more dependent on task type. A small difference in pooled rates should therefore be interpreted alongside the non-significant paired result and the subgroup reversals.')
    para('Lexical errors account for nearly half of open-ended primary spans. This pattern is compatible with uncertainty when models select specialized Kazakh terminology or translate a technical concept too literally. For example, a term referring to authority can be used where statistical representativeness is intended, or an ordinary physical sense can replace the technical meaning of a word. These errors can remain hidden inside otherwise fluent prose. Their detection requires contextual reading rather than checking suffixes or surface fluency alone.')
    para('Morphological examples involve case endings and vowel harmony, while grammatical examples involve the organization of complements and modifiers. The boundary between those categories requires careful adjudication. Kazakh permits flexible word order and omitted subjects, so borderline stylistic judgments can inflate grammatical counts. The available human confirmation expresses acceptance of the candidates but offers no separate adjudication notes. Rechecking ambiguous spans with a linguist would clarify whether the present category proportions are stable under a more conservative interpretation.')
    para('Maxutov, Myrzakhmet and Braslavski (2024) evaluated seven LLMs on several Kazakh tasks and found weaker Kazakh performance than parallel English performance, with particular difficulties in generation. Our remaining local errors are compatible with their concern about generation quality. The present experiment does not include parallel English responses and therefore cannot estimate the language gap they measured. Differences in model generations, task sets and error definitions also prevent direct numerical comparison. https://aclanthology.org/2024.sigturk-1.8/')
    para('Togmanov et al. (2025) introduced KazMMLU to evaluate Kazakh, Russian and Kazakhstan-related knowledge with educational questions. Their benchmark demonstrates the value of native-language evaluation and substantial remaining limitations. Our evidence adds a linguistic-error perspective on free-form outputs. A model can produce a grammatically acceptable response while making an incorrect factual claim, so our low primary rate cannot be read as high knowledge accuracy on KazMMLU. The supplementary factual flags illustrate this distinction. https://aclanthology.org/2025.acl-long.701/')
    para('Toleu, Tolegen and Ualiyeva (2025) studied Kazakh simplification and reported remaining tone, tense and semantic-drift errors. That work provides a relevant comparison for our lexical and morphological observations, although simplification requires preserving a source meaning under a different task objective. Our narrative and technical prompts create broader opportunities for error. Their combination of automatic metrics and native-speaker evaluation also highlights why reference overlap and internally consistent evaluator scores cannot replace recoverable independent human judgments. https://doi.org/10.3390/app15158344')
    para('Translation results illustrate a construct limitation of reference metrics. Several Claude responses add glossaries, alternatives or commentary after the requested sentence. Preserving that raw material gives a faithful evaluation of the submitted output, but it can reduce precision-based overlap even when the first sentence is reasonable. Conversely, a translation that closely resembles one reference can still misuse a domain term. BLEU, ChrF, ROUGE and contextual similarity should be read together with the linguistic examples and compliance indicators.')
    para('The practical use of these findings is targeted review of generated Kazakh. Domain terminology, case selection and formal constructions deserve attention even when an answer sounds fluent. The subgroup results suggest that the review burden depends on task type. This dataset does not measure the time, financial cost or user benefit of adopting a particular service. It therefore supports a description of observed weaknesses and a workflow for checking them, without establishing a cost-effective deployment choice.')
    heading('Threats to validity and limitations',2)
    para('Internal validity is limited by collection through different interfaces, missing timestamps and unverified model identities. Evaluation also mixes Gemini and Codex. Because GPT workbook notes mention Codex and the majority evaluator is Codex, shared-system familiarity or preference is a possible confound. The same-evaluator sensitivity check addresses mixture but cannot remove that concern. The 580 rubric evaluations were produced in this assistant session, which also prepared the report, creating additional dependence between annotation and interpretation.')
    para('Construct validity depends on the taxonomy and conservative span decisions. Supplementary-only spelling and factual problems are excluded from the primary outcome, so primary rates measure a specific linguistic construct. They do not represent all failures. Evaluator scores are subjective ordinal judgments, and their correlation with counts is partly built into the common evaluation process. The perfect agreement figures are reconstructed from a team report and must not be presented as independently observed reliability estimates.')
    para('External validity is limited by 200 custom prompts, a selected commercial-model comparison and one generation per prompt. Bootstrap intervals capture prompt sampling variation conditional on the supplied annotations. They do not quantify uncertainty over model runs, annotators or collection settings. Statistical conclusion validity is affected by many zeros and ties. The primary family and category families use the specified Holm correction, while difficulty and task analyses remain descriptive. The single-run design cannot establish temporal or stochastic stability.')
    para('A repeat study should preserve original independent labels, adjudication reasons, exact visible model identities, collection dates and generation controls. A balanced validation sample should explicitly include difficulty strata. Repeated runs would quantify stochastic variation. A separate blinded evaluator with no role in response generation would test the stability of the observed group ordering. Translation references should also receive linguistic review, since several custom source sentences and references are formulaic or awkward.')
    heading('Reproducibility statement')
